-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The condition $\min(I_p, I_q) < \text{iso}(A, B) < \max(I_p, I_q)$ is sensitive to floating-point precision. If the CT values are very close to the isovalue, numerical errors may cause missed intersections or spurious vertices [S1].
-*   **Non-manifold Junctions:** When three or more materials meet at a vertex, the resulting mesh is non-manifold. Standard rendering pipelines may struggle with non-manifold geometry. The method must ensure that each individual isosurface (e.g., Al-Air boundary) remains manifold, even if the global mesh is not [S1].
-*   **Material Classification Errors:** The accuracy of the surface extraction depends heavily on the prior material classification step. Misclassified voxels will lead to incorrect boundaries [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Classification Accuracy**: If the material classification is inaccurate, the isovalue condition may fail to detect a boundary or detect a false one, leading to gaps or artifacts in the mesh [S1].
+*   **Non-manifold Junctions**: Where more than two materials meet, the mesh topology becomes non-manifold. While the source states this is handled, standard rendering engines may require special handling for non-manifold geometry [S1].
+*   **Isovalue Selection**: The isovalue $iso(A, B)$ must be carefully selected to ensure it lies within the CT value range of both materials at the interface. If the CT values of adjacent voxels of different materials do not straddle the chosen isovalue, the boundary will not be extracted [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each cell is processed once. The complexity of the triangulation step depends on the number of material boundaries per cell.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the volume data. The output mesh size depends on the surface area of the materials.
-*   **Strengths:** Capable of extracting precise boundaries between multiple materials in a single pass (after classification). Handles non-manifold junctions naturally.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate prior material classification. The triangulation logic is more complex than standard Marching Cubes due to non-manifold cases.
-*   **When to use:** Multi-material volumetric data (e.g., CT scans of mechanical parts with different metals and air) where precise material boundaries are required.
-*   **When not to use:** Single-material data (standard Marching Cubes is simpler and sufficient).
+*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of voxels, $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the volume. Each voxel is processed once, and the number of edges per voxel is constant (12) [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of voxels for storing the input volume and output mesh. The lookup tables are constant size [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Capable of generating high-quality, precise boundary surfaces for multi-material objects (e.g., mechanical parts with different metals and air gaps) [S1]. It handles non-manifold junctions naturally [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Requires accurate pre-segmentation of the volume into material types. The method relies on the assumption that CT values are distinct enough between materials to allow for clear isovalue separation [S1].
+*   **When to Use**: When extracting surfaces from volumetric data (such as CT scans) that contain multiple distinct materials with known or estimable CT value ranges [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use**: When the data contains only a single material type (standard Marching Cubes is sufficient) or when material classification is unreliable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Generalized Marching Cubes was introduced by Suzuki et al. in 2007 as an extension of the classic Marching Cubes algorithm [S1]. It was developed to address the challenge of extracting high-quality triangular mesh models from segmented multi-material CT volumetric data of mechanical parts. The method builds on the standard Marching Cubes framework but adapts the edge evaluation and triangulation steps to handle material types and varying isovalue thresholds [S1]. It is often used in conjunction with segmentation techniques like region growing and graph-cut to classify voxels before contouring [S1].
+Generalized Marching Cubes was proposed by Suzuki et al. in 2007 as an extension of the classic Marching Cubes algorithm [S1, S2]. It was developed to address the challenge of extracting precise mesh surfaces from multi-material CT volumetric data of mechanical parts, where standard single-threshold isosurfacing fails to distinguish between different material boundaries [S1]. The method builds on the foundational work of Lorensen and Cline (1987) for Marching Cubes [S1] and incorporates concepts from dual contouring (Ju et al. 2002) to handle complex junctions [S1]. It is particularly relevant in industrial CT scanning and reverse engineering applications where mechanical parts consist of multiple materials (e.g., aluminum, steel, air) [S1].
 
